**I obtain LD* for a modified design with a random resultant multiplier. The original fixed-resultant LD* remains unproved.**

The complete proofs, exact analytic hypotheses, treatment of non-reduced classes, and referee attack are in [ldbound_report.md](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ldbound_1/ldbound_report.md). The report answers all five questions; the main findings follow.

1. **Q1 — PROVED: the proposed Farey multiplicity obstruction disappears for the actual filtered candidates.**

   Admissibility forces
   \[
   (b,g_1)=(g_1,t)=1,\qquad t=|G(P)|.
   \]
   Distinct representations of \(u=b/g_1\bmod t\) therefore give distinct real fractions. Their separation is at least \(t/M^2\), whereas their entire range has length \(O(V/M)\). Consequently their number is
   \[
   O(1+MV/t)=O(1)
   \]
   under the filter \(t\ge MV/32\). Completing \((b,g_1)\) to \((P,g)\) also has bounded multiplicity.

   Thus **FALSE for this admissible family** is the premise that arbitrarily large scalar-multiple Farey spikes remain. Without primitivity, the \(K^{-2}\) counting-measure tail does occur, and the report gives a countermodel showing an impermissible \(K\)-fold weighted-energy loss.

   Passing to \(N(b/g_1)^d\) introduces a kernel bounded by \(2d^{\omega(t)}\). A weighted version of the all-residue theorem below pays for this **inside the energy average**, with loss \(L^{o(1)}\). This proves the marginal structured-residue bound; it does not include the partner’s smoothness weight.

2. **Q2 — CONJECTURED for the original design: the remaining partner-weighted correlation.**

   On the fibre \(G(P)=\ell n\), write
   \[
   (g_0,g_1)=(g_0^*,g_1^*)+j(a,b),\qquad
   \Delta=\varepsilon\ell q.
   \]
   Then
   \[
   n'(j)=n'_0-\varepsilon qj,\qquad
   r_0(j)=N\left(\frac b{g_1^*+bj}\right)^d\bmod\ell n.
   \]
   Both quantities use the same \(j\). Averaging the coefficients does not remove this coupling.

   **PROVED barrier:** discarding the partner indicator loses
   \[
   \rho_r^{-1}=L^{(s-\nu)/(2a)+o(1)},\qquad \nu=\frac1{4k}.
   \]
   Keeping its density through divisor switching requires controlling the attached energy along that fibre. Marginal all-residue control does not supply this: energy \(1/\rho_r\) concentrated on the partner-smooth subset has bounded unweighted mean but incurs exactly that loss. Replicating this model across the outer indices defeats an inference based solely on their averaging.

3. **Q3 — PROVED: an averaged all-residue upper bound at the required precision.**

   Put
   \[
   x=N^{\xi+o(1)},\quad R=N^{r+o(1)},\quad
   Y=N^{\lambda+o(1)},\qquad \xi>r+3\lambda,
   \]
   with fixed positive margins. For \(m=\ell^2nq\), \(n\sim R\) \(B\)-smooth, \(\ell\in(Y,2Y]\) prime, and \(q\le Z\le L^C\), the report proves
   \[
   \sum_\ell\sum_{\substack{n\sim R\\P^+(n)\le B}}
   \sum_{q\le Z}\sum_{a\bmod m}\Psi(x,B;m,a)^2
   \le
   L^{o(1)}
   \frac{\Psi(x,B)^2\,\varrho(R,B)}{Y\log Y}.
   \]
   This is an upper bound at the main-square scale, **not** an \(o(1)\) relative-variance assertion.

   The proof treats induced characters explicitly, distinguishes conductors divisible by \(\ell\) from those divisible by \(\ell^2\), and divides non-reduced classes by their exact gcd. Small conductors, including a possible Siegel character, cost only \(\exp(O(\sqrt{\log N}))=L^{o(1)}\). The remaining character ranges use the precise results in [Harper, Proposition 1, Zeros Result 1(iv), and §4.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1208.5992).

   The averages over \(\ell\), smooth \(n\), and \(q\) are used in the proof; uniform fixed-index versions are not claimed. For the original parameters,
   \[
   \xi-r-3\lambda=h+(d-5)\nu>0,
   \]
   so the requested modulus range is covered.

4. **Q4 — PROVED modification: randomize the resultant multiple.**

   Use
   \[
   F=z\widehat F_g+G_gR_c,\qquad
   1\le z\le N^h,\qquad |c_i|\le C_dN^h,
   \]
   independently and uniformly, with
   \[
   h=s+\nu+\eta,\qquad \eta>0.
   \]
   Then \(F(g_0,g_1)=Nz\), so the common root modulo \(N\) remains valid. The relation image remains \(G(P)/g_1\); no \(z\)-parity column is introduced.

   Theorem R is preserved. Conditional on \(z\), Lemma Kern has exactly the same evaluation map and kernel. The report supplies a new scalar-kernel lower bound proving FM despite the larger offset, and checks irreducibility and unit-leading-coefficient restrictions.

   The budget is
   \[
   \boxed{\theta_M(d,k,\eta)=\frac3{d+1}+\frac d{4k}+\eta}.
   \]
   Thus the finite-\(k\) cost increases, while
   \[
   \inf_{k,\eta>0}\frac{1+\theta_M}{\sqrt2}
   =\frac{1+3/(d+1)}{\sqrt2}
   \]
   is unchanged. At \(d=9\), \(k=20\) permits \(c<1\) for sufficiently small positive \(\eta\); the infimum remains \(0.919239\).

5. **Q5 — PROVED LD* for this multiplier design.**

   The decisive order of operations is:
   \[
   \kappa_{\rm alg}(z)\le\frac{K_P(z)+K_{P'}(z)}2.
   \]
   Since \(N^h\gg t\) and \(\widehat F(P)\) is a unit modulo \(t\), averaging \(z\) bounds each marginal energy by
   \[
   \mathbb E_zK_P(z)
   \ll(\log N)^C\frac{t|\Delta|}{\Psi(x,B)^2}
       \sum_{a\bmod t|\Delta|}\Psi(x,B;t|\Delta|,a)^2.
   \]
   This expression depends only on \(\ell,n,q\). Divisor switching can **then** retain the partner density, uniformly on each fibre:
   \[
   \#\{g:G(P)=\ell n,\ n'\text{ smooth, filtered}\}
   \ll (M/V)\rho_r(\log N)^C.
   \]
   Summing the resulting expression with the all-residue theorem gives precisely
   \[
   \mathrm{LD*}_{M}\le L^{o(1)}S^4\rho_r^2.
   \]
   The report writes out this reduction and attacks its normalization, shared multiplier, character ranges, eligibility restrictions, and every divisor-function loss.

**Verdict:** piece (i) and marginal piece (ii) are **PROVED**; original partner-weighted LD/LD* remain **CONJECTURED**. For the multiplier design, **LD* and filtered LD are PROVED**, with budget \(\theta_M=3/(d+1)+d/(4k)+\eta\) and unchanged infimum constant. Its factoring conclusion still requires LV6 and the stated smoothness-testing guarantee, or the costlier replacement.
